<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Carniola</h1><p class="st-meta">1040 CE – 1918 CE (approx.) · capital Ljubljana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Habsburg duchy at the heart of modern Slovenia, centred on Ljubljana, a borderland between the German, Slovene and Italian worlds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> appears as a march of the Empire around 1040, a frontier zone against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Over the next two centuries it was held by various families, including the Weimar-Orlamünde counts, the patriarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Andechs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> acquired it in 1335 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV</a> called himself Duke of Carniola in 1364.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola's</a> population was mainly Slovene peasants under German-speaking nobility. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> (Laibach) was the capital. Ottoman raids struck repeatedly from the 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primoz-trubar">Primož Trubar</a> printed the first books in Slovene in 1550, and the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-weikhard-von-valvasor">Johann Weikhard von Valvasor</a> described the land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-glory-of-the-duchy-of-carniola">The Glory of the Duchy of Carniola</a> in 1689.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> from 1809 to 1813, then it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. It was a crown land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> until 1918, when it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Its core is part of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-carniola.jpg" alt="Duchy of Carniola" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Krain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1040 CE – 1918 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ljubljana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rudolf IV of Austria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Slovene, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>March of Carniola, Krain, Kranjska</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1479&amp;focus=duchy-of-carniola" aria-label="Duchy of Carniola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5023 556 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Carniola lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5343 812L5229 694M5343 812L5381 747M5343 812L5380 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5381 747h0M5380 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5343 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5343 812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ljubljana. <a href="/atlas?year=1479&amp;focus=duchy-of-carniola">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

